# Git LFS is intentionally NOT used in this repository.
#
# History: ioc/threatbase-domain.txt (~90 MiB) and ioc/threatbase-hash.txt
# (~69 MiB) were tracked in LFS until 2026-08-16. Because they are regenerated
# in full on every feed run, each run stored a complete new blob — LFS never
# deltas — which exhausted the 10 GB LFS budget in about two months.
#
# Current layout (2026-08-17):
#
#   * The unsplit threatbase-domain.txt / threatbase-hash.txt are git-ignored
#     and published as GitHub Release assets on the rolling `latest` tag, plus
#     the dated `archive-YYYY-MM-DD` tags. Release assets are billed against
#     neither the LFS storage nor the LFS bandwidth quota.
#
#   * Size-bounded chunks (threatbase-domain-01.txt, -02.txt, …) ARE committed
#     as ordinary git objects. GitHub warns above 50 MiB per file and hard-
#     rejects above 100 MiB, so update_feed.py keeps every chunk near 45 MiB.
#
# Why committing the chunks is safe, when committing the originals was not:
# both feeds are sorted and effectively append-only — a measured 786 added
# domains and 0 removed between two consecutive runs, out of 4.2M lines. Plain
# git delta-compresses that to about 14 KiB per run (measured, two real
# consecutive runs, halves committed then re-committed). LFS stored ~160 MiB for
# the same change. That is the whole difference, and it is roughly 11,000x.
#
# Rules that follow from this:
#
#   1. Do NOT add `filter=lfs` rules for any file that is rewritten on a
#      schedule. LFS is for large files that rarely change; these are the exact
#      opposite. Deleting LFS files also does not reclaim quota — server-side
#      objects persist even after history rewrites.
#
#   2. Keep the chunked feeds SORTED. The delta compression above depends on it,
#      and so does the scanner's binary search and per-chunk range routing. An
#      unsorted rewrite would both break lookups and balloon the repo.
#
#   3. Do not force text/eol conversion on the feeds. They are written with \n
#      by update_feed.py and are byte-compared during verification; a CRLF
#      rewrite on checkout would corrupt the reassembly check.
ioc/*.txt -text
